In the context of German Jewish descent, the ahnentafel system was used to identify individuals of Jewish descent, including those who were classified as Jews under the Nuremberg Laws. The system looked at the ancestry of an individual, tracing back through their grandparents, great-grandparents, and so on, to determine if they had any Jewish ancestors. If an individual had Jewish grandparents or great-grandparents, they were considered to be of Jewish descent.

