Ruston & Hornsby Ltd was formed in 1918 through the merger of Ruston, Proctor and Company and Richard Hornsby & Sons. This merger brought together two companies with complementary strengths: Ruston, Proctor and Company's expertise in steam engines and Richard Hornsby & Sons' leadership in heavy oil engines. The combined entity, Ruston & Hornsby Ltd, became a prominent manufacturer of diesel locomotives, steam shovels, and internal combustion engines.

In 1930, Ruston & Hornsby Ltd merged with Bucyrus-Erie, a US-based manufacturer of small excavators, to form Ruston-Bucyrus Ltd. This merger gave Ruston & Hornsby Ltd access to new markets and expanded its product portfolio.

However, in 1985, Ruston & Hornsby Ltd sold its share in Ruston-Bucyrus Ltd, divesting itself of non-core businesses to survive during a period of recession and consolidation in the mining industry.

Fast-forward to 1989, the company underwent a change of ownership and was renamed European Gas Turbines Ltd. In 2002, the company's operations were transferred to Siemens, and it became Siemens Industrial Turbomachinery Ltd.

