Boas' contributions to the field of cardiology were significant, and he was elected to the American Philosophical Society for his outstanding scientific contributions to the study of heart disease, particularly cholesterol and arteriosclerosis. He was also an early advocate of a universal health care system and insurance, and equal access to healthcare.

Furthermore, Boas' work in cardiology was groundbreaking, and he is credited with developing a widely used instrument for measuring heart rate. His expertise in chronic diseases of the heart and his contributions to the field of cardiology have cemented his status as a pioneer in the field of pathology and physiology.

In terms of historical context, Boas' work in the early 20th century laid the foundation for modern cardiology and paved the way for future research in the field. His development of the cardiotachometer and his expertise in chronic diseases of the heart have had a lasting impact on the field of cardiology, and his contributions continue to be recognized and celebrated today.
